<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Reform Act 1832</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1832">1832</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Great Reform Act, First Reform Act</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Reform Act 1832, known as the Great Reform Act, reformed the electoral system of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>. Passed by Earl Grey's Whig government after a political crisis and resistance from the House of Lords, it abolished many rotten and pocket boroughs, gave seats to growing industrial towns and extended the vote to much of the propertied middle class.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Reform Act</b> of 1832 abolished many rotten boroughs and gave the vote to much of the middle class; further Reform Acts in 1867 and 1884 enfranchised most male householders, the Ballot Act of 1872 introduced secret voting, and the Representation of the People Act of 1918 gave…</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom-of-great-britain-and-ireland">United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Reform Act 1832</b> failed to extend the vote beyond those who owned property, and the political leaders of the working class spoke of a great act of betrayal.</blockquote><a href="/movement/chartism">Chartism · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>When Britain’s <b>Great Reform Act</b> of 1832 widened the franchise, it defined voters as “male persons”, the first British statute to exclude women from voting in so many words.</blockquote><a href="/movement/womens-suffrage-movement">Women's suffrage movement · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Reform Act 1832</b> widened the franchise and weakened royal and aristocratic control of seats, and by the mid-19th century, after Robert Peel had failed to govern without a majority in 1834–1835, it was settled that a government defeated in the Commons must resign or call an election.</blockquote><a href="/government/parliamentary-democracy">Parliamentary democracy · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>His brother William IV succeeded him, and the <b>Great Reform Act</b> followed in 1832.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-26">26 June in history</a></li></ul></section>
